WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.650
Hi, everyone. My name is Saad Ali.

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:03.170
I'm here at KubeCon, Barcelona,

00:00:03.170 --> 00:00:06.630
and this morning I gave a keynote 
about Kubernetes storage.

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:10.500
My talk was about debunking the myth
that Kubernetes storage is hard.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.612
One of the biggest challenges
that we have with storage in general

00:00:13.612 --> 00:00:16.230
is that, really, it is complicated.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.830
But the beauty of Kubernetes
is that it introduces abstraction layers.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.770
And when you have
these abstraction layers,

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:25.030
we can take a very big,
complicated problem

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:26.810
and break it into smaller pieces.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.990
So, with Kubernetes storage,
what we're able to do

00:00:29.990 --> 00:00:32.990
is treat storage
as four different problems.

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:36.650
One, you have to figure out
what storage you want to use.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:39.420
Two, you have to figure out
how to deploy that storage.

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:43.260
Three, you have to figure out
how to integrate that deployed storage

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.160
with your Kubernetes cluster.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:48.120
Four, you have to figure out
how your containerized workload

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.440
is going to consume the deployed storage.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.930
So, to sum things up,
storage is complicated.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:57.520
But the reality is that the way
we deal with complicated problems

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.030
is by breaking them into smaller pieces.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.810
And what Kubernetes allows you to do

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:04.290
is finally treat storage
as smaller sub-problems

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:06.190
that you can handle independently.

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:08.910
And that simplifies the overall process

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.760
of deploying stateful
workloads on Kubernetes.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.270
So, for the full talk, take a look
at the description below.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.677
And stay tuned for more KubeCon content.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:20.260
♪ (upbeat music) ♪

